          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Jean Piaget's Early Life</strong><br>\nJean Piaget was born in 1896 in Neuchâtel, Switzerland. From a young age, he showed a keen interest in the natural sciences, publishing his first scientific paper on mollusks at the age of 10. He studied at the University of Neuchâtel, where he received a degree in zoology. His early academic work focused on the philosophy of science, which later influenced his psychological research.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Piaget's Theory of Cognitive Development</strong><br>\nPiaget is most renowned for his theory of cognitive development, which he believed unfolded in a series of fixed stages. According to Piaget, these stages are the sensorimotor stage, preoperational stage, concrete operational stage, and formal operational stage. He posited that children move through these stages at their own pace, developing new intellectual abilities as they do so.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Methods of Research</strong><br>\nPiaget's approach to studying children was revolutionary for his time. He used naturalistic observation, interviews, and cleverly designed experiments to probe their understanding of the world. His research often involved presenting children with problems to solve or questions to answer, then carefully noting their reasoning processes.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
