            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact on Education</strong><br>\nPiaget's work had a profound effect on education, emphasizing the importance of developmental stages in learning. His theory suggested that teaching methods and curricula should be tailored to the child's cognitive stage, allowing children to build knowledge through discovery and direct experience.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Key Concepts: Schemas, Assimilation, and Accommodation</strong><br>\nPiaget introduced the concepts of schemas, assimilation, and accommodation to explain how children adapt to new information. Schemas are mental models used to understand the world; assimilation incorporates new experiences into existing schemas, and accommodation modifies schemas when new information cannot be assimilated.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Stages of Development: Sensorimotor Stage</strong><br>\nThe sensorimotor stage occurs from birth to about 2 years old. During this period, infants learn about the world through their senses and actions. They develop an understanding of object permanence, which is the knowledge that objects continue to exist even when they cannot be seen.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
