          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Stages of Development: Preoperational Stage</strong><br>\nThe preoperational stage spans ages 2 to 7. Children in this stage begin to engage in symbolic play and learn to manipulate symbols, but they do not yet understand concrete logic. Piaget noted that children at this stage are egocentric and struggle to see things from the perspective of others.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Stages of Development: Concrete Operational Stage</strong><br>\nFrom ages 7 to 11, children enter the concrete operational stage, where they start to think logically about concrete events. They gain a better understanding of the concept of conservation—the idea that quantity does not change with the arrangement or appearance of objects—and learn to understand the perspectives of others.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Stages of Development: Formal Operational Stage</strong><br>\nThe formal operational stage begins at around 12 years of age and continues into adulthood. During this stage, individuals develop the ability to think about abstract concepts and systematically plan for the future. They can reason through hypothetical situations and use deductive logic to solve problems.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
